# Carrier Decay and Fixity
#
# How each carrier fails, from nitrate and vinegar syndrome to sticky tape, disc rot, iron-gall corrosion, and acid paper, and how checksums and copies keep digital records fixed.

#>media_carriers_decay
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!media_carriers
#:form #!ledger
#:level #!foundation
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bane
#:era #!contemporary

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Carrier Decay and Fixity"
 ~#claim: "Every carrier decays by its own chemistry, so preservation slows the chemistry with cool dry storage and checks digital copies with checksums."
 ~#proof: "Reilly and the NFPF guide on film bases; Van Bogart on tape; Byers on discs; Kolar and Barrow on ink and paper; PREMIS and LOCKSS on fixity."
 ~#door: "Read the playback DAG next, since a sound carrier is useless without its reader."
 ~#audience: "readers who keep family archives, small collections, or digital files they care about"
 continuity: "Carrier Decay and Fixity | own chemistry"
 cold_open: "Every carrier decays by its own chemistry, and a checksum is how a digital copy proves it has not drifted."
}

